WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.667
A correct-looking join key can still be wrong if it does not match the level of the relationship.

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:09.332
Suppose your target result is one row per admission.

00:00:09.332 --> 00:00:13.272
You already have patients joined to admissions correctly.

00:00:13.272 --> 00:00:17.912
Now imagine adding lab results using patient_id only.

00:00:17.912 --> 00:00:26.202
If one patient has multiple admissions and multiple lab tests, every admission can pair with every lab result

00:00:26.202 --> 00:00:29.272
for that patient. The admission grain is no longer protected.

00:00:29.272 --> 00:00:33.612
The deeper question is: what does the lab record belong to?

00:00:33.612 --> 00:00:39.682
Does it belong to the patient generally, to a specific admission, or to a specific encounter?

00:00:39.682 --> 00:00:42.287
The database design should tell you.

00:00:42.287 --> 00:00:48.552
If the true relationship uses encounter_id, joining only on patient_id is too broad.

00:00:48.552 --> 00:00:51.327
This mistake happens in many domains.

00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:56.017
A customer ID may appear in orders, payments, and support tickets,

00:00:56.017 --> 00:00:59.627
but that does not mean every payment belongs to every order.

00:00:59.627 --> 00:01:07.652
A student ID may appear in registrations and grades, but the correct relationship may also require course_id

00:01:07.652 --> 00:01:14.067
and term. Before adding a table, restate the target grain and ask whether the new table is at the same level.

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.640
If not, aggregate it, filter it, or use a more specific key.
